<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- TWO entitlements, and together they are the whole of what this process may
     do. The macOS extension is held to the same shape as the iOS one, and every
     absence below is load-bearing rather than merely unclaimed.

     `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is not optional here the way it is for a
     Developer ID app: an appex runs inside another application's session and
     macOS requires it to be sandboxed.

     `com.apple.security.application-groups = [7PVYUG4YQS.com.relayium.shared]`
     gives the extension a directory the containing app can also read. That is
     the entire hand-off: copy what the user shared into that directory, write a
     small plan describing it, and say on the sheet that the files are waiting in
     Relayium. Nothing is pushed to the app — a Share extension may not open its
     containing app — so what brings the draft onto a send surface is the user
     opening Relayium themselves.

     **The identifier is team-prefixed and is NOT iOS's string.** iOS uses
     `group.com.relayium.app`; the macOS provisioning profiles authorize
     `group.com.relayium.shared` plus the wildcard `7PVYUG4YQS.*`, and Apple
     documents the macOS form of an App Group as `<team>.<group>`. It must stay
     byte-identical to `AppGroup.macOSIdentifier`, and `MacSurfaceGuardTests`
     compares the two — a mismatch is the failure that looks like everything
     working, where the sheet stages a draft into one container and the app lists
     an empty one.

     No `keychain-access-groups`. The extension holds no bearer, reads no
     account, and generates no content key. The key for a shared draft is minted
     in the APP when the user presses Send. A keychain group here would be a
     credential share with a process that has nothing to share, and would put the
     user's bearer one API call away from a target whose whole safety argument is
     that it cannot reach it.

     No `com.apple.developer.associated-domains`. The extension neither receives
     a link nor emits one.

     No `com.apple.security.network.client` and no `network.server`. This target
     is a staging process: it copies plaintext the user chose into the App Group
     and stops. Encryption, the account's limits, the upload and the link are all
     the app's and stay the app's — and with no network entitlement at all, that
     is enforced by the sandbox rather than by review.

     No `files.user-selected` either: what this process may read is what the
     Share menu hands it, and nothing else. -->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>7PVYUG4YQS.com.relayium.shared</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
